  "query_preview": "..\n\nFast west-northwest upper flow remains directed across the Great \nLakes tonight through Thursday as the synoptic pattern holds \nrelatively stagnant over much of the continent. This maintains a \nmodestly unstable environment with periodic chances for shower and \nthunderstorm activity each day, but details on timing and placement \nwill continue to be governed by smaller scale perturbations within \nthe upper flow. Thus, this part of the forecast continues to carry \nrelatively low confidence for the local area. The greater threat of \nsevere weather and heavy rainfall will continue to be favored over \nthe Ohio Valley through midweek.\n\nFor the near term, a mature MCV is driving decaying convection \nacross northern OH along a CAPE gradient reinforced by remnant \noutflow from a morning MCS. The orientation of this gradient and \nCorfidi vectors favor further ESE propagation to continue along this \ncorridor to our south. The northern edge of remnant convection is \ncurling across areas south of I-94 with an isolated threat of a \ngusty winds to 40 mph and localized heavier rainfall, but this \nthreat is rapidly sinking south of the border. Otherwise, stratiform \nrain passes across areas south of I-96 over the next few hours with \nupstream observations indicating potential for a few embedded \nlightning strikes.\n\nAfter the MCS departs, outflow on the northern flank meets a diffuse \nstationary front positioned across southern lower MI and may provide \nthe trigger for additional isolated to widely scattered smaller \nscale and disorganized convection south of I-69 the rest of the \nafternoon. The thick cirrus shield associated with the MCS has \ndampened afternoon destabilization, but latest mesoanalysis \nadvertises 500 to 1000 J/kg of MLCAPE given sfc dew points in the \nlower 70s and mid-level lapse rates of 6.5 to 7 C/km. The main \nwindow for convection will be through about 8pm with any lingering \nactivity tapering off by 10pm. Included a patchy fog mention tonight \nas the front lingers in the area with higher humidity and clearing \nskies.\n\nSubtle height falls on Wednesday cause a weak surface \ntrough/convergence axis to sink south from northern Lower into the \nSaginaw Valley and Thumb vicinity during the afternoon. This may be \nenough to trigger convection, and with the active jet overhead there \nwill be a threat for isolated strong to severe wind or hail as well \nas a localized downpour. Hi-res ML guidance favors a window between \n3pm and 9pm for any stronger convection. The favored area for \nconvection on Thursday looks to again set up south of the area \ntoward the Ohio Valley.\n\nBy late Thursday into Friday, the large scale pattern will begin to \nevolve with the western CONUS ridge amplifying over the Rockies. \nSurface high pressure builds across northern Ontario on Friday then \nmigrates south into the Great Lakes to provide more tranquil and \nslightly cooler weather through most of Saturday. This is followed \nby a more potent system set to track across the Great Lakes on \nSunday. Its warm front arrives late Saturday, bringing a chance of \nshowers and storms by late evening. Ensemble QPF output indicates a \nthreat for heavy rainfall somewhere in the vicinity - 13Z NBM \nadvertises 30% for over 1 inch for parts of Lower MI by 00z Mon.",
